      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
          "content": "Subjects with asthma frequently have nasal symptoms and complain of orthopnoea but airflow resistance is usually only assessed during oral breathing and while seated."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHOD",
          "content": "We have used a forced oscillation technique to measure total respiratory resistance (Rrs) at 6Hz during mouth breathing (Rrs,mo) and during nose breathing (Rrs,na) in the sitting and supine postures; resistance of the nasal airway (Rnaw) was estimated as Rrs,na--Rrs,mo. Forced oscillations were applied during normal tidal breathing and the mid-tidal lung volume (MTLV) was determined for each breathing route and posture."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SUBJECTS",
          "content": "Three groups of subjects were studied: 10 normal subjects without lung or nasal disease (N; five males, mean age 33.5 [range 23-58] years, mean FEV1 105%pred, FEV1/VC 86%); seven subjects with asthma alone (A; four males, 40.3 [23-57] years, mean FEV1 66%pred, FEV1/VC 74%); 10 asthmatic subjects with nasal obstructive symptoms (AN; six males, 62.8 [38-80] years, mean FEV1 56%pred, FEV1/VC 75%)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In all three groups of subjects, mean Rrs,mo and Rrs,na were higher in the supine than sitting posture. In normal subjects the increase in supine Rrs,mo was associated with a 0.6 liter fall in MTLV. In asthma supine Rrs,mo increased despite a much smaller fall in MTLV; supine increases in Rrs,na were particularly large in presence of nasal disease."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "DISCUSSION",
          "content": "Values of airflow resistance are 2-3 times higher in both normal and asthmatic subjects when breathing via the nose and supine than under normal laboratory conditions of oral breathing and seated."
        }
      ]},
